---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
---

<Base title="About" description="What this journal is, and how Tori serves it.">
  <h1 class="post-title">About</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A travel journal of slow trains and long walks in Central Europe. The trips, and the people met on them, are made up;
    the site is real, and it is built with Astro.
  </p>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>How it is served</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>astro build</code> turns the Markdown posts into one HTML page each, a page per tag and an RSS feed, all
        in <code>dist/</code>. Tori serves those files as they are.
      </li>
      <li>
        No page loads any JavaScript. Astro sends none unless a component asks for it, and nothing here does, so the
        pages are as quick as plain HTML because they are plain HTML.
      </li>
      <li>
        Each page is a directory with an <code>index.html</code>, like <code>/tags/rail/</code>, so an address works
        with or without its last slash.
      </li>
      <li>Nothing runs on a server for it, so it never naps.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>Deploy your own</h2>
  <p>With Node.js 22.23.4 or newer:</p>
  <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/tori-host/examples
cd examples/drift-albatross
npm install
npx tori.host</code></pre>
  <p>
    With no <code>dist/</code> yet, the CLI runs <code>npm run build</code> and publishes <code>dist/</code>. The first
    run signs you in, creates a site under a new name of its own and writes that name to <code>tori.json</code>.
  </p>
</Base>
